Volume = {47},
Number = {2},
Pages = {259-268},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {The seas surrounding India, namely Arabian Sea (AS) and Bay of Bengal
   (BoB) with their associated coastal embayments form one of the highly
   productive areas and biodiversity hotspots in the tropics contributing
   profusely to the socioeconomic front of the region. Therefore, acquiring
   knowledge on the climate change scenario of this region and its impacts
   on marine ecosystems in general and planktons, in particular, is
   considered crucial for better resilience. In fact, several attempts have
   been made of late to understand the climate change impacts on plankton,
   corals and mangroves of this region. In this article, we tried to update
   the climate change scenario of Indian seas and its impact on plankton
   communities based on the information gathered from the peer reviewed
   publications and scientific reports. Results of this review have shown
   that the global warming generated SST (Sea Surface Temperature) rise and
   sea water acidification related pH fall have affected the species
   composition, abundance, phenology and metabolic pathways of plankton
   populations in this region.},
